le burst  and gave us a very severe recession  that was also really the story of the  2001 recession that we had the stock  bubble burst and  uh technically that's not considered a  serious recession i considered a bad one  because if you look at the labor market  we continue to lose jobs the recession  officially ends in december of 2001  we continue to lose jobs all through  2002 and most of the way through 2003  and we didn't get back the jobs we lost  until january 2005. so  to my view that was a serious recession  but long and short when we're looking  for cues  you know advanced warning the two  stories one do we have a bubble that's  about to to burst  and for that you know i don't i don't  see any bubbles there driving the  economy and i did see both the stock  bubble and housing bubbles so  you know i do look for things like that  i don't see anything there now  the other story is the fed raising  interest rates aggressively well  we never got the interest rate all that  high no donald trump was yelling about  it but  you know the interest rate the fed's  interest rate never got above two and a  half percent that just lowered it twice  i i don't see either story of a  recession so the classic  um causality for a recession the fed or  a bursting bubble it's just not there so  i i really don't see one on the horizon  looking forward the topic of automation  is getting more and more attention when  it comes to the presidential race uh  it's been mostly uh andrew yang who has  been talking about it and i've read some  pieces  saying that this is an overblown fear  that in the same way that in the past  technology has destroyed some jobs and  industries but always created more  that that will continue to happen on the  other hand there are  papers i've read which say the rate at  which  that has happened in the past is  changing and over time  technology will destroy more jobs than  the number it creates  possible to know that at this point in  time what's your sense of the importance  of this automation issue  well what i find ironic and i just did a  little paper looking at productivity and  the share portion of productivity growth  that's going to to workers  and first point out is we've actually  we're actually a period of  extraordinarily weak productivity growth  so for all the talk of automation  if you actually look at the data we're  actually losing jobs to technology at a  historically low rate you know i know  all these people i know a lot of people  push that argument they go we have all  this great technology and  maybe it is great but the fact is it's  not affecting the economy  so the idea that we're about to lose all  these jobs to technology  i just see basically zero basis for that  and  if it did happen i mean if we were to  see a more rapid rate of productivity  growth that would be great that would  create a possibility of more rapid wage  growth we could work fewer hours  i just i this is store a story with  basically no reality whatsoever to it so  it's not one of the things that keeps me  up at night what is the thing that keeps  you up at night when it comes to the  economy  well i'd have to say with the economy uh  i don't know what you want to call the  economy or not i mean global warming is  a huge huge  issue and you know we're doing nothing  on it i mean uh  you know donald trump seems to think  it's a good thing to put his  throw as much greenhouse gases into the  environment as possible  and unfortunately the us is still such a  big actor in the world that  if we just ignore any efforts to reduce  greenhouse gas emissions it makes it a  really big problem and of course it  makes you know other countries you know  obviously are looking to us and not you  know you could say as a leader but the  other point is  well if we're out there spewing as much  greenhouse gases as we can into the  atmosphere  probably doesn't make sense for you know  france or germany or  uk to do anything because it won't  matter um so  that that is you know to my view far  away the most pressing thing and i'm  hugely concerned about inequality and  you know what bothers me is we don't  discuss the issue the right way i was  going on about patents and copyrights  i raised this with economists and they  look at me blank-faced it's not that i  think economists are brilliant people  but this is their topic  and the idea that we can't you know they  they'll give me this story about  oh offered income upward redistribution  of income that's unfortunate but that's  just technology  and they go well suppose we do have  patents and copyrights and then look at  me  well if we didn't pay some copyrights  we'd have no incentive i go okay fine  but then you just told me that's a  policy so  you know but anyhow there's a tendency  just to treat it as this blob that like  oh  we have inequality and it's really  unfortunate even people on the left i  mean they want to have  progressive taxes which i think are  great but they  treat the underlying cause of inequality  as being something that's intrinsic to  the market  as opposed it was how we structured the  market yeah and it's amazing how i mean  when you talk about economics  invariably climate change comes up when  you talk about national security  invariably climate change comes up and  it is a it's an issue with uh  far far reaching tentacles for sure um  we've been speaking with economist dean  baker who's a senior economist at the  center for economic and policy research  also a visiting professor at the  university of utah  dean i really appreciate your time today  thanks a lot for having me on i really  enjoyed the discussion
 

